  "core_relation": {
    "sentence": "The persuasiveness of the resurrection case depends on the convergence of multiple independent lines of evidence—early pre-Pauline creeds, the admitted eyewitness status of Paul, the bodily-resurrection testimony of both friendly and critical scholars, and the historical reliability of the sources—such that removing any one line weakens but does not by itself destroy the cumulative argument.",
    "quote": "What keeps the creeds from being expanded until they become myth? And they're not really the words of the eyewitnesses. Critics admit that Paul was an eyewitness. They admit that Paul's their hero, good guy. They like them. And they always grant those se first the seven books. [S401-S407]"
  },
  "product_test": {
    "answer": "additive",
    "quote": "So, if it if it can be taken either way in context, why don't you find five more places where I said it the the way you're blaming me with? Instead of using one cloudy one, why don't you give me five more clear ones? [S317-S318]",
    "reason": "Habermas's argument is explicitly cumulative: multiple early creeds, multiple sources, multiple scholars across the spectrum. He repeatedly asks for 'five more' instances rather than treating a single counterexample as fatal. The conclusion (resurrection is historically knowable) would be weakened but not zeroed by losing one line of evidence. The paper shows no single ingredient whose absence collapses the entire case."
  },
  "slots": [
